// isolate-cancel — **the honest report.** `cancel` is a *request*; `join` reports what actually
// happened. A request that arrives after the work is already done changes nothing, and `join` says
// `Ok(v)` — never `Err(Cancelled)` for work that ran to completion.
//
// `quick` needs one round to finish, and the top-level `sleep(5).await` drives the scope's tasks,
// so by the time `h.cancel()` runs the task has a stored result. Cancelling it is a no-op and the
// join reports the value. (The same rule is what the real-thread isolate path had to be rebuilt to
// obey: it used to latch "cancelled" the moment it asked, and reported that for a worker that then
// ran to completion anyway — see `crates/noeta-cli/tests/cli/isolates.rs`.)
// expect: stdout "quick ran"
// expect: stdout "ok=7"
// expect: exit 0
use std.task.{sleep}
async fn quick(): int {
    echo "quick ran"
    return 7
}
concurrent {
    h = spawn quick()
    sleep(5).await
    h.cancel()
    echo match h.join() {
        Ok(v)  => "ok=" ~ v,
        Err(_) => "cancelled",
    }
}
